---
import Guide from '@/layouts/Guide.astro'
import CodeSwitcher from '@/components/CodeSwitcher.astro'
import TerminalCast from '@/components/TerminalCast.astro'
import Callout from '@/components/guides/Callout.astro'

const install = `npm install ai @ai-sdk/openai @ai-sdk/otel zod \\
  @arizeai/openinference-vercel \\
  @opentelemetry/sdk-node @opentelemetry/exporter-trace-otlp-proto`

const setup = `// tracing.ts: import this once, before the first AI SDK call.
import { NodeSDK } from '@opentelemetry/sdk-node'
import { OTLPTraceExporter } from '@opentelemetry/exporter-trace-otlp-proto'
import { OpenInferenceBatchSpanProcessor } from '@arizeai/openinference-vercel'
import { registerTelemetry } from 'ai'
import { LegacyOpenTelemetry } from '@ai-sdk/otel'

export const sdk = new NodeSDK({
  serviceName: 'support-agent',
  spanProcessors: [new OpenInferenceBatchSpanProcessor({ exporter: new OTLPTraceExporter() })],
})
sdk.start()

registerTelemetry(new LegacyOpenTelemetry())`

const env = `# The base URL. The exporter adds /v1/traces itself.
export OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:4317
# Only when mcpd runs with --ingest-key.
export OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS="Authorization=Bearer YOUR_INGEST_KEY"`

const call = `import { sdk } from './tracing.ts'
import { generateText, stepCountIs, tool } from 'ai'
import { openai } from '@ai-sdk/openai'
import { z } from 'zod'

const { text } = await generateText({
  model: openai('gpt-4.1-mini'),
  system: 'You answer questions about orders.',
  prompt: 'Where is order 4417?',
  tools: {
    lookupOrder: tool({
      description: 'Look up where an order is.',
      inputSchema: z.object({ orderId: z.string() }),
      execute: async ({ orderId }) => ({ orderId, depot: 'Leeds' }),
    }),
  },
  stopWhen: stepCountIs(5),
  // Names the run: the root span becomes "ai.generateText support-agent".
  telemetry: { functionId: 'support-agent' },
})
console.log(text)

// A script exits before the batch is sent unless you shut down. A server does not need this.
await sdk.shutdown()`

const readBack = `TRACE=$(curl -s "http://localhost:4317/v1/api/traces?limit=1" | jq -r '.traces[0].trace_id')
curl -s "http://localhost:4317/v1/api/traces/$TRACE" \\
  | jq -r '.spans[] | [.name, .kind, .model, .usage.input_tokens, .cost.total_cost] | @tsv'`
---

<Guide
  slug="trace-vercel-ai-sdk"
  lede="The AI SDK records its calls as OpenTelemetry spans. Register its telemetry, add the OpenInference span processor, and export to mcpd: every generateText call arrives as a run with its prompt, tool calls, tokens and cost."
  related={['log-openai-api-calls', 'trace-chat-conversations', 'llm-cost-per-user']}
>
  <p>
    When you finish, each <code>generateText</code> call is one trace in mcpd:
    the call, every model step with its input, output, tokens and cost, and every
    tool call with its arguments and result. You need Node.js 22 or later, AI SDK
    7, and a running mcpd daemon on port 4317 (see the
    <a href="/docs/quickstart" class="link">Quickstart</a>).
  </p>

  <h2>1. Install the packages</h2>
  <p>
    <code>@ai-sdk/otel</code> holds the AI SDK's OpenTelemetry integration.
    <code>@arizeai/openinference-vercel</code> rewrites its spans in the
    OpenInference conventions, which is where mcpd finds each step's input and
    output.
  </p>
  <CodeSwitcher class="not-prose my-6" title="install" samples={[{ lang: 'curl', label: 'Shell', shiki: 'bash', code: install }]} />

  <h2>2. Register the telemetry</h2>
  <p>
    Start the OpenTelemetry SDK with the OpenInference processor in front of the
    OTLP exporter, then register the AI SDK's <code>LegacyOpenTelemetry</code>
    integration. Telemetry is on for every call once an integration is
    registered.
  </p>
  <CodeSwitcher class="not-prose my-6" title="tracing.ts" samples={[{ lang: 'typescript', label: 'TypeScript', shiki: 'typescript', code: setup }]} />
  <Callout>
    Use <code>LegacyOpenTelemetry</code>, not <code>OpenTelemetry</code>. The newer
    integration repeats the token counts of every model call on the root
    <code>invoke_agent</code> span. mcpd adds up the usage of every span in a run,
    so the run's tokens and cost would count each call twice.
  </Callout>

  <h2>3. Point the exporter at mcpd</h2>
  <p>
    The OTLP exporter reads its endpoint from the environment. Name port 4317:
    mcpd serves OTLP there, and the HTTP exporter would otherwise try 4318.
  </p>
  <CodeSwitcher class="not-prose my-6" title="environment" samples={[{ lang: 'curl', label: 'Environment', shiki: 'bash', code: env }]} />

  <h2>4. Name the call</h2>
  <p>
    Your calls need no change. Add <code>telemetry.functionId</code> so the run
    has a name you can filter on. Without it the root span is named
    <code>ai.generateText</code> for every call.
  </p>
  <CodeSwitcher class="not-prose my-6" title="agent.ts" samples={[{ lang: 'typescript', label: 'TypeScript', shiki: 'typescript', code: call }]} />

  <h2>5. Read the trace</h2>
  <p>
    Open <a href="http://localhost:4317" class="link" rel="noopener">localhost:4317</a>
    and select the newest run in <strong>Runs</strong>, or read it from the API:
  </p>
  <CodeSwitcher class="not-prose my-6" title="read the run" samples={[{ lang: 'curl', label: 'Shell', shiki: 'bash', code: readBack }]} />
  <TerminalCast
    class="not-prose my-8"
    title="one generateText call with a tool"
    lines={[
      { cmd: "curl -s \"http://localhost:4317/v1/api/traces/$TRACE\" | jq -r '.spans[] | [.name, .kind, .model, .usage.input_tokens, .cost.total_cost] | @tsv'" },
      { out: 'ai.generateText support-agent\tagent_run\tgpt-4.1-mini\t\t' },
      { out: 'ai.generateText.doGenerate\tmodel_generation\tgpt-4.1-mini\t812\t0.000384', tone: 'ok' },
      { out: 'ai.toolCall\ttool_call\t\t\t' },
      { out: 'ai.generateText.doGenerate\tmodel_generation\tgpt-4.1-mini\t812\t0.000384', tone: 'ok' },
    ]}
  />
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>AI SDK span</th><th>mcpd kind</th><th>What it holds</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>ai.generateText</code></td><td><code>agent_run</code></td><td>The whole call: the prompt and the final text</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>ai.generateText.doGenerate</code></td><td><code>model_generation</code></td><td>One request to the provider, with model, tokens and cost</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>ai.toolCall</code></td><td><code>tool_call</code></td><td>One tool execution, with its input and result</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The cost of the run is the sum of its model steps. Each step is priced from
    its token counts and mcpd's price book, as
    <a href="/docs/cost" class="link">Cost accounting</a> explains.
  </p>

  <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>What you see</th><th>Why, and what to do</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>The run's tokens and cost are twice the sum of its model steps</td>
        <td>The process registered <code>OpenTelemetry</code> from <code>@ai-sdk/otel</code>. Register <code>LegacyOpenTelemetry</code> instead.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Spans arrive with an empty input and output</td>
        <td>
          The OpenInference processor is missing, so the prompt stays in AI SDK attributes such as
          <code>ai.prompt.messages</code>. They are under <strong>Attributes</strong> in the span panel,
          but full-text search does not read them.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>A prompt served from the provider's cache costs as much as an uncached one</td>
        <td>
          The AI SDK reports cached tokens as <code>ai.usage.cachedInputTokens</code>, a name mcpd does
          not read yet. mcpd prices those tokens at the full input rate, so the figure is an upper
          bound when caching is on.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>No trace arrives from a script</td>
        <td>The process exited first. Call <code>await sdk.shutdown()</code> before it ends.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>The exporter logs 401 or 403</td>
        <td>The daemon has ingest keys. Set <code>OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS</code> as in step 3.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</Guide>
